    {
      "slug": "05_reddit-gym-autorenew-contract",
      "script": "Paragraph nine did WHAT? Our gym's new manager quietly auto-renewed annual contracts to juice his retention numbers, then routed cancellations through a desk open only two weekday mornings a month. I got charged for a year I never agreed to, so I reread my signed contract. Paragraph nine said the gym had to send certified-mail notice sixty days before renewal—or the renewal was void. They'd sent nothing. I wrote a demand letter quoting the clause and the state's consumer protection statute, then copied corporate legal. Within a week, I had a refund and an apology call. Corporate checked the whole chain, found over two thousand members caught by the same silent renewal, and refunded roughly three hundred eighty thousand dollars. The manager's retention bonus was clawed back that quarter. Still proud of paragraph nine?",
      "editor_note": "Tightened to 134 words, added a four-word question hook, front-loaded paragraph nine, and ended on the specific clause without changing claims or shot structure."
    },
